WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.020
The safest sanitizer fix usually removes the unsafe assumption that caused the report.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:15.400
It is tempting to make the smallest local edit, but a good remediation asks why the program was able to perform an invalid operation in the first place.

3
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:24.860
The answer may involve ownership, lifetime, bounds, initialization, numeric ranges, or an API contract that was too vague.

4
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:35.300
For memory-access findings, consider whether raw buffers should become standard containers, strings, spans, or other views that preserve size information.

5
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.800
Keep pointer and length data together.

6
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:43.420
Validate sizes before indexing, copying, parsing, or allocating.

7
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:55.120
If the finding involves stale access, clarify who owns the object, shorten borrowed lifetimes, and use RAII so cleanup follows object lifetime rather than manual release paths.

8
00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:00.040
For undefined behavior findings, look at type choices and preconditions.

9
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:09.340
Signed overflow, invalid shifts, questionable casts, and alignment assumptions often point to design that needs clearer bounds or stronger representation.

10
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.280
Initialize data deliberately.

11
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.460
Avoid states where every caller must remember a hidden setup rule.

12
00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:20.920
Prefer conversions and range checks that make invalid input explicit.

13
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.960
After the fix, add regression coverage around the corrected behavior.

14
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:29.860
Rerun the sanitizer configuration and review the change with the report in mind.

15
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.080
The goal is not only to make the current report disappear.

16
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:40.540
The goal is to make the same class of defect less likely to return through a nearby path, future refactor, or similar interface.

17
00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:44.180
Be careful with fixes that only add a guard near the crash site.

18
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:56.440
Sometimes that is appropriate, but often the better repair is higher in the design: validate an input once, preserve length with the data, transfer ownership explicitly, or reject invalid state before it enters a component.

19
00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:01.180
The closer the fix is to the broken assumption, the more durable it usually becomes.
